# Docker Compose environment for the weftd stack.
#
# Compose reads this automatically (`docker compose up -d`). It holds the Postgres
# password and which ports get published. Everything else is application config in
# weft.toml / livekit.yaml.
#
# The password is NOT copied into weft.toml: weftd expands `${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}`
# from the environment when it loads that file, so this is the only place it is
# written. An unset value fails the start instead of becoming an empty password.

# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Read by the postgres service and passed to weftd.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-postgres-password

# ── Published ports ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# weftd's HTTP surface (the web client, /ws, /.well-known/weft, /media) and
# LiveKit's signalling are plain HTTP/WS. Who needs to reach them decides the bind
# address:
#
#   0.0.0.0     (default) reachable from the network AND from containers in the
#               other stacks — which is what ../caddy needs, since it reaches this
#               stack through the host rather than a shared Docker network.
#               Plain HTTP is then exposed to anyone who can route to this host:
#               FIREWALL these two ports, or use the option below.
#   172.17.0.1  the Docker bridge address. Reachable by containers, not from
#               outside — tighter, but check the address matches your Docker
#               network (`ip -4 addr show docker0`).
#   127.0.0.1   loopback only. Fine if nothing else is containerised, but Caddy
#               in another project then CANNOT reach it.
WEFT_HTTP_BIND=0.0.0.0
WEFT_HTTP_PORT=8081
LIVEKIT_BIND=0.0.0.0
LIVEKIT_PORT=7880
